1: # The marked common exterior of the three cusp double curves
2: 
3: Working reduction for the remaining finite-isotropy fan branches.
4: This gives the actual integral groups and inclusion maps of the
5: common exterior, but does not identify the relative Pontryagin
6: four-cycle of a second edge.
7: 
8: Let \(C=C_0\cup C_1\cup C_2\subset S^6\) be the three marked
9: double curves of the index-one cusp. Each \(C_i\) is a complex
10: projective line, and all three meet at the same two triple
11: points. The local maps are the three coordinate axes in the
12: two smooth charts with base monomial \(q=x_0x_1x_2\).
13: Take small coordinate balls at the two vertices and tubes
14: along the six intervening complex arcs, with the normal
15: orientations induced by the complex charts. Their rounded
16: regular neighbourhood is \(N(C)\); put
17: \(E_C=S^6\setminus\mathring N(C)\).
18: 
19: Give each sphere two oriented edges \(a_i,b_i\) joining the
20: two marked vertices and two oriented faces with boundaries
21: \(a_i-b_i\) and \(b_i-a_i\). This is an actual CW structure on
22: the union, with differential matrices
23: \[
24:  C_2=\mathbb Z^6\xrightarrow{D_2}C_1=\mathbb Z^6
25:      \xrightarrow{D_1}C_0=\mathbb Z^2,\qquad
26:  D_1=\begin{pmatrix}-1&-1&-1&-1&-1&-1\\
27:                      1&1&1&1&1&1\end{pmatrix},
28: \]
29: where \(D_2\) consists of three diagonal blocks
30: \(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&-1\\-1&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)\).
31: The three sums of the two faces are the oriented classes
32: \([C_i]\). The two marked one-cycles are
33: \(a_1-a_0,a_2-a_0\). The program below checks that these
34: are full primitive bases, not finite-index sublattices.
35: Thus
36: \[
37:        H_2(C)=\mathbb Z^3,\qquad H_1(C)=\mathbb Z^2.
38: \]
39: 
40: Triangulate the two coordinate-ball models and their tubes
41: compatibly with this CW subdivision. The ordinary dual-cell
42: construction in the oriented six-sphere identifies the
43: reduced complement chains with the shifted cochains of
44: \(C\): a \(j\)-cell is intersected once by its oriented
45: normal dual cell. This is the Alexander-duality chain map
46: in the present marking, with no torsion quotient. It gives
47: \[
48:  H_3(E_C)=\mathbb Z^3,\qquad H_4(E_C)=\mathbb Z^2,
49: \]
50: and no other positive reduced homology. The three
51: degree-three generators are the positive normal meridians
52: of the \(C_i\); they have linking number \(+1\) with the
53: corresponding complex sphere and zero with the other two.
54: The degree-four generators are the dual-cell cycles of the
55: two displayed one-cycles. This also specifies them at the
56: triple-point charts, where a bare product description of a
57: rim would be insufficient.
58: 
59: The complement is simply connected by relative general
60: position: a loop bounds a disc in \(S^6\), and its disc can
61: be made disjoint from the two-dimensional strata and the
62: two zero-dimensional strata, with its boundary fixed.
63: The same argument transports through the regular-neighbourhood
64: retraction to \(E_C\).
65: 
66: For a chosen curve \(C_i\), let
67: \(E_i=S^6\setminus\mathring N(C_i)\). The exact inclusion
68: \(E_C\hookrightarrow E_i\) fills the other two tubular
69: pieces and the corresponding vertex collars. In the
70: meridian bases its map on \(H_3\) is the literal coordinate
71: projection
72: \[
73:                   (m_0,m_1,m_2)\longmapsto m_i,
74: \]
75: because normal meridians of either filled sphere bound
76: their normal four-discs. Its \(H_4\) map is zero.
77: Haefliger's unknottedness result already verified in K23
78: identifies \(E_i\) with \(D^3\times S^3\), with that marked
79: normal meridian. The exact relative sequence therefore gives
80: \[
81:  H_5(E_i,E_C)=\mathbb Z^2,\qquad
82:  H_4(E_i,E_C)=\mathbb Z^2,
83: \]
84: and no other positive relative group. The first boundary
85: map is the isomorphism onto \(H_4(E_C)\), and the second
86: identifies with the kernel spanned by the other two
87: meridians in \(H_3(E_C)\).
88: 
89: Small meridian representatives for the three distinct
90: curves are pairwise disjoint. Hence the absolute
91: three-dimensional intersection map of \(E_C\) vanishes.
92: Poincare--Lefschetz duality and the boundary sequence give
93: \[
94:  H_1(\partial E_C)=\mathbb Z^2,\quad
95:  H_2(\partial E_C)=\mathbb Z^3,\quad
96:  H_3(\partial E_C)=\mathbb Z^3,\quad
97:  H_4(\partial E_C)=\mathbb Z^2.
98: \]
99: The maps in degrees three and four to \(E_C\) are
100: isomorphisms in the above dual-cell bases.
101: 
102: The still required comparison is precise. The selected
103: edge's relative doubled \(D^3\times S^1_{\rm parameter}\)
104: four-cycle must be transported through the two displayed
105: relative groups, with the actual Cox normal frames at both
106: triple points. The Thom identity of
107: notes/part3_equivariant_thom_work.md gives its local
108: Pontryagin change after that pullback; it does not choose
109: the pullback. Nor does the vanishing of the absolute
110: Pontryagin class choose it. The integer relative
111: coordinates \(m=-k+6A\) and \(n=-6A\) are both retained.
112: 
113: The exact cellular certificate is
114: code/part3_fourfold/three_curve_exterior.py and its JSON.
